The two files in src/vendor/ are copied verbatim from the bambuddy server's
frontend. src/entry.js is ours; it wires them into the page the app loads.
`npm run build` writes two assets: assets/gcode/viewer.js (that bundle) and
assets/gcode/fonts.css (the app's own faces, subset — see the section below).

    source:  https://github.com/maziggy/bambuddy
             frontend/src/lib/gcodeToolpath.ts       → src/vendor/gcodeToolpath.ts
             frontend/src/lib/vendor/toolpathRenderer.js → src/vendor/toolpathRenderer.js
    commit:  7c117dc6bc5ffef66aa81b8ea23619a386e22285
    date:    2026-08-10
    version: APP_VERSION = 1.2.6b1

toolpathRenderer.js is itself vendored by the server, from `three-slicer@0.1.1`
(https://github.com/kimgh06/Web_Three_Slicer), which ports OrcaSlicer's
`libvgcode` — the renderer the desktop slicer draws its own preview with. That
lineage is why a plate here looks like a plate in Studio rather than an
approximation of one. All of it is AGPL-3.0-or-later, as is this app; the
bundled output carries the same licence.

Both files are copied rather than depended on: the npm package behind the
renderer carries an 8 MB WASM slicing kernel and pins its own three.js version,
and the parser is a single module with no package at all.

## Why the app carries this at all

The preview used to be a page served by bambuddy itself. The server deleted it
in the 1.2.6 cycle and answers the old path from its SPA catch-all with a 200,
which left the app on a black screen with no error to show (issue #17). Drawing
the toolpath in the app removes the dependency on any server page: what is left
is `GET /library/files/{id}/gcode` and `GET /archives/{id}/gcode`, plain API
routes that predate both viewers and outlived one of them.

The trade this makes is deliberate and worth remembering: an upstream fix to the
parser or the renderer no longer reaches users through a server update. It
reaches them when someone re-vendors here and ships a release.

## To re-vendor

The maintainer's server clone lives at reference/bambuddy:

    cp reference/bambuddy/frontend/src/lib/gcodeToolpath.ts \
       tool/gcode_viewer/src/vendor/
    cp reference/bambuddy/frontend/src/lib/vendor/toolpathRenderer.js \
       tool/gcode_viewer/src/vendor/

then update the commit/date/version above and run, from this directory:

    npm ci
    npm run check     # the copies still read a BambuStudio plate
    npm run build     # regenerates assets/gcode/viewer.js and fonts.css

`npm run check` is the tripwire for a copy that landed truncated or from a
version whose annotations moved — it parses a plate written the way BambuStudio
writes one ("; FEATURE:", "; CHANGE_LAYER", "; Z_HEIGHT:", G2 arcs) and asserts
the layer count, the line width and the bounds.

Commit the regenerated `assets/gcode/viewer.js` and `assets/gcode/fonts.css`
with the sources, the way the `*.g.dart` files are committed: CI has no node,
and the assets are what ship. (`.gitignore` inherits a blanket `*.js` from
Flutter's dart2js boilerplate, so the bundle and this directory's JavaScript
are re-included by name at the bottom of that file. Renaming any of them
without updating those lines drops them from the repository silently.)

## Rebuilding without the server clone

Nothing here needs the clone except the copy step — the sources in src/vendor/
are complete. `npm ci && npm run build` reproduces the asset from what is in
this repository.

## The fonts

The app draws in Manrope and JetBrains Mono, both bundled in the APK. The page
cannot use them from there: it is loaded with the *server's* base URL, so a
relative `url()` asks the user's bambuddy for a file it has never heard of, and
`file:///android_asset` is cross-origin from an http document. Either the faces
travel inside the document or the preview renders in whatever the system
dresses the WebView in.

So `build.mjs` subsets them and writes `assets/gcode/fonts.css`, which Dart
inlines at the `__BB_FONTS__` marker. Two things keep the cost sane:

- **only the faces the stylesheet asks for** — Manrope 400/600/700 and
  JetBrains Mono 400/700. Adding a weight to `viewer.html` means adding it to
  `FACES` in `build.mjs`, or the browser synthesises it.
- **only the characters the page can show** — ASCII, Latin-1, Latin Extended-A,
  and the punctuation the labels use. Whole, the five faces would add ~700 KB
  to every document; subset and woff2-compressed they add ~160 KB.

Latin Extended-A is what carries the Polish letters, so a new locale on the
Latin script needs no change here. **A locale outside it — Cyrillic, Greek —
would render in the system face**: legible, not ours, and no error anywhere to
tell you. Widening the range in `build.mjs` and rebuilding is the fix.

The `–` in the range readout ("12–47") is deliberately in the kept set; it is
easy to lose when trimming further and it shows up as a blank box.
